\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Disambiguation neglect and prompt-level safety.}
The 4.6\% awareness rate implies prompt-level safety instructions are insufficient. Authorization scope responds to explicit disambiguation ($-$18.0\,pp), but incompleteness does not ($-$3.4\,pp, n.s.) because missing information cannot be recovered from the prompt alone (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}).

\paragraph{Convergent nocuity and agreement-based evaluation.}
Convergent nocuity operates at two levels. At the \emph{practical} level, 41.3\% of ambiguous clauses trigger majority-convergent violations---regardless of whether this convergence arises from independent high violation rates or shared bias, multi-agent consensus is unreliable as a safety proxy for these clauses. At the \emph{mechanism} level, the observed convergence exceeds the Poisson binomial independence baseline by $+$8.3\,pp (95\% CI $[2.2, 14.3]$; excluding incompleteness; Appendix~\ref{app:nocuity_sans_incomp}), and cross-family Jaccard ($0.46$; Figure~\ref{fig:convergent_nocuity}) approaches within-family levels ($0.54$), indicating shared architectural biases beyond independent vulnerability. The practical implication is immediate: agreement-based monitoring cannot detect the most systematic failures, because the failures themselves are convergent.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
The type--mechanism dissociation maps types to targeted mitigations: numerical thresholds for authorization scope ($-$36.0\,pp), structural disambiguation for scopal, and default-value specification for incompleteness. Effect magnitudes range from $+$12.7\,pp under naturalistic conditions (SOPBench) to $+$58.8\,pp in a healthcare pilot, consistent with the upper/lower bound framing in which $\tau^2$-bench ($+$34.0\,pp) provides a controlled upper bound.

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

Our ambiguity pairs are synthetically constructed, so the 34.0\,pp effect is an upper bound; the SOPBench replication ($+$12.7\,pp unweighted mean) provides a cross-benchmark lower bound. We evaluate seven API-accessible models across two customer-service domains at temperature $= 0$; a healthcare cross-domain pilot ($+$58.8\,pp, $n = 250$; Appendix~\ref{app:healthcare}) confirms the effect generalizes beyond customer service, though smaller models remain untested. Failure mode coding relies on LLM annotators ($\kappa = 0.52$; human $\kappa = 0.32$, rising to $0.41$ after collapsing the spurious-compliance/structural-misparse boundary; improved guidelines, prior round $0.15$), though the coder prompt excludes ambiguity type labels, preventing type--mechanism contamination. All experiments use temperature $= 0$, measuring each model's modal interpretation---the policy-relevant quantity agents will most likely commit to and the typical deployment setting for policy-governed agents. The matched-pair design partially mitigates temperature effects: any variance increase at higher temperatures applies to both conditions, so the treatment effect direction is unlikely to reverse. Moreover, the unambiguous-condition violation rate is already near floor (6.9\%), so temperature-induced variance would predominantly affect the ambiguous condition, preserving or widening the gap. A temperature $= 0.7$ pilot across three model families (GPT-4.1, Claude Sonnet~4.6, DeepSeek-V3; Appendix~\ref{app:temperature}) confirms that the treatment effect is robust (85--97\% clause-level agreement with greedy decoding), though the remaining two models and extreme-temperature regimes remain untested; the per-model pilot ($n = 50$ pairs) is powered to detect effects $\geq$24.6\,pp at 80\% power, so smaller attenuations at higher temperatures cannot be ruled out. Three additional scope limitations warrant disclosure: (1) three of the five judges share the OpenAI model family; however, GPT-5.4-judged and GPT-4.1-judged models show similar mean violation rates (38.0\% vs.\ 45.3\%, driven by DeepSeek's high base rate rather than judge leniency; cross-model judge $\kappa = 0.653$, McNemar $p = 0.606$), suggesting the 3:2 allocation does not systematically inflate convergence estimates; (2) authorization scope has the lowest per-type judge agreement ($\kappa = 0.385$, $n \approx 17$), so conclusions about this type carry wider uncertainty; (3) the coreferential type, whose variants differ only in referent specification, was excluded from both the mitigation and SOPBench replication analyses, limiting generalization for that type; and (4) the ICC analysis uses only within-family variation (GPT-4o/4o-mini), so the reported reliability bounds may not hold across architecturally diverse model pairs. Future work should extend to non-customer-service domains and collect per-episode human mechanism annotations to validate the type--mechanism association.

\section*{Data and Code Availability}
The 300 clause pairs, evaluation pipeline, analysis scripts, and raw episode traces will be released upon publication at \url{https://github.com/anonymous-nlp-2026/policy_ambiguity_safety}.

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

A customer asks to return a customized item within the 30-day window. The agent consults the store policy, which states that ``items may be returned within 30 days.'' The word ``items'' scopes over all product categories, including custom orders that are normally final sale. The agent grants a full refund and arranges free return shipping. One word of scopal ambiguity costs the retailer \$200.

LLM-based agents increasingly operate under natural-language policy specifications that govern their permitted actions. These specifications inevitably contain ambiguity---a pilot audit of 407 clauses from 24 production policy documents finds a majority of clauses contain at least one ambiguity type (55--87\%, depending on union vs.\ intersection of two judges; $\kappa = 0.30$; Appendix~\ref{app:prevalence})---a property studied for decades in requirements engineering (RE) and computational linguistics. \citet{berry_kamsties_2004} distinguish between \emph{nocuous} ambiguity, where different stakeholders arrive at different interpretations, and \emph{innocuous} ambiguity, where shared context ensures convergent interpretation. We document a distinct failure mode that breaks this assumption: LLM agents \emph{converge} on the same incorrect interpretation, making ambiguity harmful through systematic, undetectable error rather than inconsistency (\S\ref{sec:analysis}). We term this \emph{convergent nocuity}: Berry's criterion---predicated on divergent interpretations---does not extend to LLM agents, where convergence reflects shared training biases rather than shared domain knowledge, and errors are invisible to agreement-based monitoring.

LLM agents lack these disambiguation channels. They cannot consult colleagues, rarely escalate to users, and must resolve ambiguity in real time from the text alone. This raises a question that has not been empirically addressed: does policy ambiguity cause safety violations in LLM agents, and if so, does the \emph{type} of ambiguity predict the severity of the violation? Prior work classifies ambiguity types in policies \citep{drip_r_2026,li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024} or measures policy understanding quality \citep{policybank_2026}, but no existing study provides a causal, type-granularity mapping from ambiguity to safety violations.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_overview.pdf}
\caption{Experimental overview. (a)~Six-type ambiguity taxonomy (two layers) with 300 matched clause pairs. (b)~Matched-pair causal design: each clause pair is injected into $\tau^2$-bench policy documents; five models interact under both ambiguous and unambiguous conditions across 2,997 episodes. (c)~Cross-model judge evaluation classifies violation severity; key findings show a $+$34.0\,pp violation increase from ambiguity and convergent nocuity across models.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

We address this gap with a matched-pair causal study on $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark. Using a six-type taxonomy organized into two layers---linguistic and specification (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy})---we construct 300 ambiguous/unambiguous clause pairs and evaluate five architecturally diverse models across 2,997 episodes.

Our results reveal a \emph{type--mechanism dissociation}: policy ambiguity consistently increases violations ($+34.0$\,pp under synthetic conditions; $+31.0$\,pp excluding incompleteness), but ambiguity \emph{type} predicts \emph{how} agents fail, not \emph{whether} they fail. Clause wording explains 22.4\% of violation variance while type membership explains only 0.4\%; yet type predicts failure mechanism substantially more strongly than violation rate (NMI $= 0.23$ vs.\ $0.01$; Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.45$ vs.\ $0.14$ on tables of different dimensionality; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}). This dissociation suggests the taxonomy may function as a diagnostic instrument rather than a risk classifier (\S\ref{sec:discussion}). Incompleteness is the highest-risk type (46--62\%); the remaining types form a partial ordering with robust extremes but margin-dependent mid-tier structure (Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity}).

Our contributions are: (1)~the first matched-pair causal study establishing that policy ambiguity increases violations by $+$34.0\,pp ($+$31.0\,pp excluding incompleteness; $+$18.7\,pp for the confound-minimized coreferential type), replicated on SOPBench ($+$12.7\,pp unweighted mean; all five $\Delta > 0$), with clause wording---not type membership---as the primary risk factor among measured predictors (22.4\% vs.\ 0.4\% of variance; \S\ref{sec:results}); and (2)~\emph{convergent nocuity}---a failure mode in which 41.3\% of ambiguous clauses trigger majority-convergent violations across architecturally diverse models (Jaccard $= 0.46$ cross-family vs.\ $0.54$ within-family), with 77\% permissive bias, rendering agreement-based monitoring blind to systematic failures (\S\ref{sec:analysis}). An exploratory type--mechanism dissociation is reported in \S\ref{sec:analysis}.

